     I think one of the big problems with the display is treatment of blind
characters, especially with telepathy:
1. "It hits!"  Need I say more?
2. I might note that you can "C" while hallucinating, and thus determine
   if a monster is your pet.  It'd be more fair to say "You couldn't
   recognize whatever you plan to name."
3. Even if you don't have telepathy, "You are hit from the northeast!"
   would be more appropriate.  If you do, of course, it should simply
   name the monster.
4. If you or a monster move over an object, it is not left on the display
   (as when not blind), and in fact if you previously knew about it, it
   disappears.  I don't see the rationale for this.
5. If you push a boulder, you logically know where it's going to end up.
   No reason why it should disappear from the display.
6. If you run into a wall, you similarly know it's there; it should use a
   turn and appear on the display.
7. Searching should be drastically impeded, as someone previously mentioned.

     This would make it slightly more difficult to play while blind, but
also a helluva lot more convenient display-wise.  The player experiences
several drawbacks related to convenience when blind with telepathy, such as
having objects erased from the display (exception: if you're represented by
the cursor only, rather than a @.  i.e. invisible and not see-invisible.
Monsters still erase objects from the display, even if not in the same room,
if you have telepathy.)  On the other hand, you experience almost none of
the realistic drawbacks such as running into walls.
     Also, perhaps when you're displaced, your true location should be
displayed by the cursor and your apparent one by a @ (in some random
direction, one square away).  This would jive with being represented by
a cursor while not able to see oneself; the @ represents where you see
yourself (if you do) and the cursor where you know yourself to be based on
surroundings.

     An alternative approach to being blind, also a major handicap, is to
completely erase the map, as does Moria.  So being blind and telepathic
would be like an at-will detect monsters spell, but not worth much more.
It would also eliminate the effective immunity from yellow lights that
you get once you eat a floating eye.
     This could be partially counteracted by an intrinsic, perhaps, but
I'm not so sure how.
     (Erasure of map is temporary, of course; it comes back when you can
see.)
     Oh, one last note: "You can't see in here!" when engulfed by a vortex.
What the *hell* does this mean?  Why would it have anything to do with
the few turns after your expelled until it goes away?


